    "created_at": "2020-05-31T16:52:11Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-05-31T16:52:25Z",
    "body": "> > It could be a good idea to just use\r\n> > `ghc --version`\r\n> > This might not grab the current project version, but it'll grab the current ghc compiler version instead. A lot faster, about `0.236 total` from `time ghc --version`\r\n> > If you'd like I can implement this quick and dirty workaround to grab the version number from `ghc`?\r\n> \r\n> I'm not Haskell/Stack user but this approach feels like a regression to me. Please correct me if this is not the case but I would assume most users would want to know what version of `ghc` a project is using rather than the global version. I'm assuming the workflow would be similar to a Python developer using starship to show which version of Python they are using to know what language features they can or cannot use. Is this the sort of workflow that most of our users are using? Again I'm not a haskell user so maybe this doesn't matter and the global version of `ghc` is fine?\r\n\r\nI believe you're right (as a Haskell/Stack user).",
